<div class="interactive-textbook">

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Introduction</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      Let's explore the visual effects of changing the driving frequency in a forced harmonic oscillator. We'll observe how the system's behavior changes as we vary the frequency of the external force.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Key Concepts</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      A forced harmonic oscillator is a system that oscillates with a natural frequency but is also subjected to an external, periodic force. The system's response depends on the relationship between the driving frequency and the natural frequency. Key concepts include:
      <ul>
        <li><b>Natural Frequency (ω₀):</b> The frequency at which the system oscillates freely.</li>
        <li><b>Driving Frequency (ω):</b> The frequency of the external force.</li>
        <li><b>Amplitude:</b> The maximum displacement of the oscillator.</li>
        <li><b>Resonance:</b> The phenomenon where the amplitude is maximized when the driving frequency is close to the natural frequency.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Formula</div>
    <div class="formula-card">
      <div class="formula-card-title">Equation of Motion</div>
      <div class="formula-card-formula">
        $$
        m\frac{d^2x}{dt^2} + b\frac{dx}{dt} + kx = F_0\cos(\omega t)
        $$
      </div>
      <div class="formula-card-explanation-button">[ Explain Formula ]</div>
      <div class="formula-card-lab-button">[ Open Formula Lab ]</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Visual Effects and Resonance</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      As the driving frequency (ω) increases in a forced harmonic oscillator, the visual effect changes significantly.
      <ul>
        <li><b>Low Driving Frequency (ω << ω₀):</b> The oscillator moves in sync with the external force but with a smaller amplitude. The displacement lags the force by a small amount.</li>
        <li><b>Driving Frequency Approaching Natural Frequency (ω ≈ ω₀):</b> The amplitude of the oscillation increases dramatically. This is called resonance. The system absorbs the maximum energy from the driving force, and the displacement lags the force by approximately 90 degrees. Visually, the oscillator's amplitude becomes very large.</li>
        <li><b>Driving Frequency Exceeding Natural Frequency (ω >> ω₀):</b> The amplitude decreases. The oscillator's motion becomes less responsive to the driving force, and the displacement lags the force by almost 180 degrees. The oscillator's response becomes small and out of phase with the driving force.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Graphical Representation</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      The relationship between the driving frequency and the amplitude can be represented graphically.
      <div class="table-card">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Driving Frequency (ω)</th>
              <th>Amplitude</th>
              <th>Phase Difference</th>
              <th>Visual Effect</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>ω << ω₀</td>
              <td>Small</td>
              <td>Small lag</td>
              <td>Oscillator moves with the force, small amplitude.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>ω ≈ ω₀</td>
              <td>Large (Resonance)</td>
              <td>~90 degrees lag</td>
              <td>Large amplitude oscillations.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>ω >> ω₀</td>
              <td>Small</td>
              <td>~180 degrees lag</td>
              <td>Small amplitude, out of phase with the force.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Important Notes</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li>Damping (represented by the 'b' term in the equation) affects the sharpness of the resonance peak. Higher damping leads to a broader, less pronounced peak.</li>
        <li>The amplitude at resonance is inversely proportional to the damping coefficient.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Summary</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      Changing the driving frequency of a forced harmonic oscillator dramatically alters its behavior. Resonance occurs when the driving frequency is near the natural frequency, resulting in large amplitude oscillations. Increasing the driving frequency beyond the natural frequency leads to a decrease in the amplitude and a phase shift.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Suggested Questions</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li>How does damping affect the resonance frequency and amplitude?</li>
        <li>What are some real-world examples of forced harmonic oscillators and resonance?</li>
        <li>How can you experimentally determine the natural frequency of an oscillator?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>
```

